{"qid":"Q504759","names":["Chu"],"title":"Chu","endonym":null,"capsule":"Chu was the largest territorial state of the Chinese Warring States period, occupying most of the middle and lower Yangtze valley (modern Hubei, Hunan, Anhui, and adjacent regions) with a cultural tradition distinct from the northern Zhou states. The state traced its royal lineage back to the early Zhou period (11th century BCE) but expanded dramatically from the 7th to 4th centuries BCE under kings such as Zhuang (r. 613–591 BCE), who briefly dominated the central plains. Chu was notable for its distinctive lacquerware, silk textile traditions, and Daoist-influenced literature (the Chu Ci poetry anthology), reflecting a southern cultural sphere separate from Confucian orthodoxy. Repeated Qin military campaigns under general Wang Jian culminated in the conquest and annexation of Chu in 223 BCE, leaving only a brief southern resistance before the Qin unification of China.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core Chu territory in the Yangtze basin is attested by bronze inscriptions, excavated administrative wooden slips (bamboo-strip documents from Chu tombs), and transmitted historiography; the outer frontier zones, especially the northern expansion into the Huai River basin, are less precisely mapped and rely on received texts (Shi Ji, Zhan Guo Ce) combined with archaeological find distributions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Chu (state)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chu_(state)"}],"britannica":"place/Chu-ancient-state-China-770-223-BC","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Chu (state)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":